cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  The Serving Chain: the architecture of a modern token system, once the weights
  are available
message: >-
  If you use this work, please cite both the paper and the software. Once a
  Zenodo DOI is minted, add it under `identifiers` below and to the README.
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Hariprasad
    family-names: Sudharshan
    # Add your ORCID here once you have one — it takes two minutes at orcid.org
    # and it is what links this record to you permanently across venues.
    # orcid: 'https://orcid.org/0000-0000-0000-0000'
repository-code: 'https://github.com/brettleehari/tokencalci'
url: 'https://tokencalci.onrender.com'
abstract: >-
  When a laboratory publishes model weights, the field treats the difficult part
  as complete. A weight file is an artefact; a served token is a system. This work
  contributes a decomposition of the intervening work into ten layers, with an
  inclusion test, a cost attribution, and an argument about which layers an
  enterprise can acquire and which it cannot. Exactly one of the ten arrives in
  the download. Nine layers can be bought, built or hired; utilisation cannot,
  because it is a property of serving many uncorrelated tenants rather than of
  engineering competence. Priced across five representative models at the
  precision providers actually serve, bare compute runs 0.15x to 1.80x the
  neocloud rate while fully loaded cost runs 1.7x to 8.2x — the premium is created
  by the operational layers, not by hardware. Accompanied by an open calculator,
  a reconstructed 19-point per-model price series recovered from a package's git
  history, and a script that regenerates every figure in the paper.
keywords:
  - large language models
  - inference serving
  - open weights
  - total cost of ownership
  - GPU economics
  - tensor parallelism
  - quantisation
  - vLLM
  - capacity planning
